 \revred{Table~\ref{tab:expert_new_agreement} reports independent judgments before adjudication. For E1/E2 on the 24 new positives, mean Jaccard similarity is 0.670 for minimal evidence sets and 0.723 for repair sets. Agreement involving E3 is conditional on the selected subset and does not estimate agreement among three raters over all cases. Across all 42 cases, E1/E2 agreement is high for conflict existence, while sufficiency has a lower $\kappa$, distinguishing the two annotation tasks.}
 
 \paragraph{Expert-reference scoring.}
-\revred{For a system output $H$, minimal-evidence recall is $|H\cap M|/|M|$, repair coverage is $|H\cap R|/|R|$, and repair-reference precision is $|H\cap R|/|H|$. The outside-reference fraction, $|H\setminus R|/|H|$, measures disagreement with the repair reference; it does not establish that unreferenced nodes are irrelevant. We use E3's complete reference for the five adjudicated positives. For the other 19, we score against E1 and E2 separately and average their scores within each case, without merging their reference sets. All positive reference denominators are nonempty.}
+\revred{Following ERASER's distinction between human-rationale agreement and faithfulness to model predictions \citep{deyoung2020eraser}, our expert-reference metrics evaluate agreement with annotated evidence. For a system output $H$, minimal-evidence recall is $|H\cap M|/|M|$, repair coverage is $|H\cap R|/|R|$, and repair-reference precision is $|H\cap R|/|H|$. The outside-reference fraction, $|H\setminus R|/|H|$, measures disagreement with the repair reference; it does not establish that unreferenced nodes are irrelevant. We use E3's complete reference for the five adjudicated positives. For the other 19, we score against E1 and E2 separately and average their scores within each case, without merging their reference sets. All positive reference denominators are nonempty.}
 
 \revred{Predictions are the returned evidence core for \samcgs{}, the fixed-cap LEA output, and the direct One-shot subgraph. For \samcgs{}, we use \texttt{core\_evidence\_risk\_subgraph\_nodes}, which is available at inference time, and exclude diagnostic fields that use ground truth. Failed cases remain in the denominator. Invalid or empty outputs receive zero recall, coverage, and precision. Their outside-reference fraction is also zero by convention, so this metric must be interpreted alongside validity.}
 
